WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.350
Last, a light that changes color to warn you.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.425
Look at the diagram.

3
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:08.100
Some lights have colored sectors.

4
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:12.950
A red sector usually covers a hazard, like a shoal or a reef.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.525
So if you’re watching a white light and it turns red, what’s it telling you?

6
00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:20.625
You’re standing into danger.

7
00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:22.800
Turn back toward the white.

8
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.975
Now the exam trap.

9
00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:30.075
The Light List gives the edges of each sector as bearings in degrees true.

10
00:00:30.325 --> 00:00:31.950
But from where?

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.925
From the vessel, looking toward the light.

12
00:00:35.175 --> 00:00:37.350
Not from the light looking out.

13
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.200
People get that backward all the time.

14
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.225
One last trick.

15
00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:47.850
The moment a light first pops up over the horizon, you’re at its geographic range.

16
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.600
So you know roughly how far off you are.

17
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.000
That’s called bobbing a light.

18
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.150
That’s lesson five.

19
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.050
Time the period.

20
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.225
Lamp, air, earth.

21
00:00:59.475 --> 00:01:02.725
One point one seven times the square root.

22
00:01:02.975 --> 00:01:05.300
And red sector means danger.

23
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.625
Next time: the books every captain keeps.
